WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.667
ELT.

2
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:06.300
Swap the last two letters of ETL, and you stop throwing data away.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.933
ELT loads the raw data first, untouched, then transforms it inside the warehouse.

4
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:19.333
Modern warehouses have cheap, elastic compute, so the old reason to transform outside is gone.

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.907
In ETL, the transform code owns the truth.

6
00:00:22.907 --> 00:00:24.933
In ELT, the raw data does.

7
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:30.600
And since raw data is kept forever, when history matters, ELT is the safer default.

8
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.033
So the order swaps.

9
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.067
We still extract from the source.

10
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.800
But we load straight away, raw and untouched.

11
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.933
Only then do we transform, inside the warehouse.

12
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:44.067
This is the dbt pattern.

13
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:47.233
At the bottom sit raw tables, landed untouched.

14
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:50.867
On top, versioned SQL views clean and type the data.

15
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.119
And above those, models the analysts query.

16
00:00:54.119 --> 00:00:59.200
Anyone can read and review that SQL in a pull request, no Python required.

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.600
Here it is in code.

18
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.300
First, we land each payload as raw JSON, exactly as it arrived.

19
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.800
Then the transform is just a view inside the warehouse.

20
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.344
If the view has a bug, we fix the SQL and rebuild.

21
00:01:13.344 --> 00:01:14.967
The raw rows never moved.

22
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:18.267
Here is a case where ELT shines.

23
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:23.233
A space-weather API is flaky: fields appear, vanish, and change shape.

24
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:28.500
With ETL, every surprise crashed the nightly job and lost that night's data.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:36.200
With ELT, the raw JSON always lands, and a parsing bug becomes a view fix and a rebuild over all history.

26
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.244
Let's recap.

27
00:01:38.244 --> 00:01:42.533
ELT loads raw data first and transforms inside the warehouse.

28
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:45.033
Raw data stays as the source of truth.

29
00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.867
Transforms are versioned SQL that anyone can audit.

30
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.603
When history matters, ELT is the safer default.

31
00:01:52.603 --> 00:01:53.700
Next up: Spark.
